      modelAnswer: 'Dijkstra\'s algorithm finds the shortest path from a chosen starting node to all other nodes in a weighted graph, where each edge has a non-negative weight representing, for example, distance, time or cost. It works by gradually building up the shortest known distance to each node from the start. The algorithm keeps a record, for each node, of the shortest distance found to it so far from the start; initially the start node\'s distance is set to zero and every other node\'s distance is set to infinity (unknown), and all nodes are unvisited. It then repeats the following process: it selects the unvisited node with the smallest known distance from the start (initially the start node itself) and marks it as the current node. For the current node, it examines each of its neighbouring unvisited nodes and calculates the distance to each via the current node — that is, the current node\'s distance plus the weight of the edge to the neighbour. If this calculated distance is less than the neighbour\'s currently recorded distance, the neighbour\'s distance is updated to this smaller value (this is called relaxation), and the current node is recorded as the way to reach it. Once all the current node\'s neighbours have been considered, the current node is marked as visited and will not be checked again, because the shortest distance to it is now known. The algorithm then again selects the unvisited node with the smallest recorded distance and repeats, continuing until all nodes have been visited (or until the destination is reached). When it finishes, the recorded distance to each node is the length of the shortest path from the start, and the recorded predecessors can be followed back to reconstruct the actual paths. The algorithm works because, by always choosing the nearest unvisited node, it can be sure that node\'s shortest distance has been found. In explaining its use, Dijkstra\'s algorithm is widely used for finding shortest or least-cost routes, such as in satnav and mapping systems and in network routing, wherever the shortest path through a weighted network is needed and edge weights are non-negative.'
