      modelAnswer: 'Big-O notation describes how an algorithm\'s resource requirements — usually running time, but also memory — grow as the size of the input grows, expressing the dominant rate of growth (such as O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n) or O(n²)) while ignoring constant factors and lower-order terms. It is widely used to compare algorithms, and its strengths in this role are significant. It provides a machine-independent measure of efficiency: because it describes how an algorithm scales rather than its exact timing on a particular computer, two algorithms can be compared without depending on the hardware, language or implementation used to run them. It captures what matters most for large inputs — the growth rate — so it clearly shows, for example, that an O(n log n) sort will vastly outperform an O(n²) sort as the data grows, allowing developers to predict scalability and choose algorithms that will remain practical on large inputs. It is concise and abstracts away unimportant detail, making it a convenient common language for reasoning about and discussing efficiency. However, in assessing its use, there are important limitations. Big-O describes asymptotic behaviour — how things grow for large n — so it can be misleading for small inputs, where constant factors and lower-order terms (which it ignores) may dominate; an algorithm with a worse Big-O can actually be faster than one with a better Big-O on small data. By discarding constant factors, it can hide real and significant differences: two O(n) algorithms may differ greatly in actual speed. It usually describes the worst case, which may rarely occur in practice, so an algorithm with a poor worst case may perform well on typical data (and best-case or average-case analysis may be more relevant). It does not account for real-world factors such as how well an algorithm uses the cache or exploits parallel hardware, which can strongly affect actual performance, nor does it directly measure other concerns such as ease of implementation. In assessing its overall value, Big-O notation is a very useful and important tool for comparing algorithms: it gives a clear, machine-independent indication of how they scale, which is essential for choosing algorithms for large inputs and is one of the foundations of algorithm analysis. But it should be used with awareness of its limitations — it is most reliable for large inputs and for comparing different growth rates, while for small inputs, for distinguishing algorithms with the same Big-O, or for predicting exact real-world performance, it must be supplemented by considering constant factors, typical-case behaviour and actual measurement. The fair assessment is therefore that Big-O is an indispensable but not complete tool: excellent for understanding and comparing scalability, but not a substitute for practical testing and judgement when precise or small-scale performance matters.'
