      modelAnswer: 'Traversing a graph means systematically visiting all its nodes, and the two main traversal algorithms — breadth-first search (BFS) and depth-first search (DFS) — differ in the order in which they visit nodes and in the data structures they use, which makes each suited to different purposes. Breadth-first search explores the graph in layers, visiting all the nodes at the current distance from the starting node before moving on to nodes further away. Starting from a chosen node, it visits all its immediate neighbours first, then all of their unvisited neighbours, and so on, spreading outward level by level. BFS uses a queue (a first-in, first-out structure) to keep track of the nodes to visit next: the start node is added to the queue, and repeatedly the node at the front is removed, its unvisited neighbours are marked as visited and added to the queue, until the queue is empty. Depth-first search instead explores as far as possible along one path before backtracking. Starting from a node, it follows one neighbour, then a neighbour of that node, and so on, going deeper and deeper until it reaches a node with no unvisited neighbours, at which point it backtracks to the most recent node that still has unvisited neighbours and continues. DFS uses a stack (last-in, first-out), either explicitly or via recursion (which uses the call stack), to remember where to backtrack to. In discussing their uses, the difference in traversal order makes each better for particular tasks. BFS, because it explores in order of increasing distance from the start, is well suited to finding the shortest path (in terms of the number of edges) between two nodes in an unweighted graph, since it reaches nearer nodes first; it is used, for example, in finding shortest connections in networks and in problems where the nearest solution is wanted. DFS, because it goes deep along paths, is well suited to problems such as exploring or exhausting all possibilities along paths, detecting cycles, finding connected components, topological sorting of dependencies, and solving puzzles such as mazes where one path is followed until it fails and then another is tried (it naturally supports backtracking). In terms of memory, BFS can use a lot of memory on wide graphs because the queue may hold many nodes at a level, while DFS\'s memory use relates to the depth of the path. In discussing them together, BFS and DFS are complementary: both visit every node and can be used to search a graph, but BFS explores broadly level by level using a queue and is suited to shortest-path-in-edges and nearest-first problems, while DFS explores deeply along paths using a stack or recursion and is suited to exhaustive exploration, cycle detection, and backtracking-style problems. The choice between them depends on the task — whether the nearest nodes or a deep exploration of paths is wanted — and on the structure of the graph and the memory available, which is why both are fundamental algorithms studied and used widely in computer science.'
