      modelAnswer: 'Both Dijkstra\'s algorithm and the A* (A-star) algorithm find the shortest path through a weighted graph, and A* can be understood as an improvement on Dijkstra\'s algorithm for the common task of finding the shortest route from a start to a specific goal. Dijkstra\'s algorithm works by always expanding the unvisited node with the smallest known distance from the start, gradually building up the shortest distance to every node. It considers only the distance already travelled from the start (often called g, the cost so far), and because it explores outward in all directions in order of distance from the start, it can examine a great many nodes that lead away from the goal before it reaches the goal, which is inefficient when only the path to one particular destination is needed. A* improves on this by also using an estimate of how far each node is from the goal, so that it preferentially explores nodes that appear to be heading toward the goal. For each node, A* calculates a value f = g + h, where g is the actual cost of the path from the start to that node (as in Dijkstra), and h is a heuristic estimate of the remaining cost from that node to the goal (for example, the straight-line distance to the goal on a map). At each step, A* expands the unvisited node with the smallest f value — that is, the node that appears to offer the lowest total estimated cost of a path through it to the goal — rather than simply the node nearest the start. The effect is that A* focuses its search toward the goal, examining far fewer irrelevant nodes than Dijkstra\'s algorithm and so finding the shortest path to the goal more quickly. Crucially, provided the heuristic is admissible — meaning it never overestimates the true remaining cost to the goal — A* is guaranteed to find the shortest path, just as Dijkstra\'s does, but more efficiently; a good heuristic that closely estimates the remaining distance makes A* much faster, while if the heuristic is zero, A* effectively becomes Dijkstra\'s algorithm. In explaining the improvement, then, A* enhances Dijkstra\'s algorithm by adding a heuristic estimate of the distance still to go, combining the actual cost so far with this estimate to guide the search toward the goal; this means A* explores far fewer nodes and reaches a specific destination more quickly than Dijkstra\'s algorithm, while still guaranteeing the shortest path when the heuristic does not overestimate. This makes A* the preferred choice for pathfinding to a known goal, such as in games, robotics and route planning, while Dijkstra\'s algorithm remains appropriate where shortest paths to all nodes are needed or where no useful heuristic is available.'
